- **Demo:** draws the edges as `#`, the glyph its shared map code treats as open [[c08a:96-113 backend:54-65]].
- **Ours:** follows the talk’s words and makes them walls [[own]].

## Step through it
@@steps limit=6

## Controls
@@control seeds
Seed points scattered [[c08a:21-27]].
@@control metric
The distance that decides a tile’s nearest seed. The demo shows all three and builds its edges from the straight-line one [[c08a:36-53 c08a:57-73 c08a:77-93 c08a:96-110]].
@@control edges
`walls`: the talk’s demo. `roads`: our own rule for the roads the talk describes [[own]].

## What it produces, measured
@@measured

- **Walls:** seal each cell. {{c08a.regions.median}} regions at the median; the probe finds no path on any of the {{c08a.path.nulls}} seeds [[run:c08a.regions.median c08a.path.nulls]].
- **Roads, ours:** 1 region on {{roads.regions.is1}} of 200 seeds, 2 on the other {{roads.regions.is2}} [[run:roads.regions.is1 roads.regions.is2]].
  - **Why the lower-right neighbor:** without it, an edge that runs down and to the left touches itself only at corners. That seals a cell, but can’t be walked [[own]].

## Where it fails
@@fails

- **Read with care:** these are failures only for a map meant to be walked whole. Sealed cells are what the wall rule is for [[own]].

## What it combines with
@@combines

- **Talk’s use, roads:** its author used the edges as roads in a game, and filled each cell with a kind of district [[t:142-145]].
- **Talk’s use, spawning:** monsters that belong together in one cell; ones that must stay apart in faraway cells [[t:139-141]].

